WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
WALMART SALARIES:
Salaries at Walmart have become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.480
insane. Software engineers for example can expect&nbsp;
to earn as much as half a million per year. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:15.660
such salaries aren’t too crazy at big tech&nbsp;
companies and VC funded startups but Walmart isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:22.320
either of those. They’re a retailer, a legacy&nbsp;
retailer. For perspective, the top engineers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:29.940
at Target earn over $200,000 less at $278,000.&nbsp;
And at Costco, the average software engineer is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.100
barely able to cross a $100,000. This doesn’t&nbsp;
just apply for software engineers either. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:41.040
product managers, they’re offering up to $500,000.&nbsp;
For engineering managers, they’re offering up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:48.060
$600,000. And even for data scientists, they’re&nbsp;
offering up to $400,000. In fact, Walmart is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.880
paying so much that for certain tech job titles,&nbsp;
they’re literally on the leaderboard for paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:01:00.120
the highest compensation. Take Solution Architect&nbsp;
for example. Walmart comes in as the 24th highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:07.620
paying company for this position with a median&nbsp;
of $223,000. This places them just $2,000 behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:13.380
Apple and $3,000 behind Facebook. This also places&nbsp;
them ahead of some of the largest tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:19.680
out there including Cisco, Salesforce, Oracle,&nbsp;
and Dell. We should also note that Walmart is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.780
the only retailer that makes the leaderboard other&nbsp;
than Amazon of course but they’re more of a tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.900
company than a retailer. Walmart on the other hand&nbsp;
is definitely still more of a retailer but they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:36.360
leaning into tech more than ever. In fact, they&nbsp;
have a full on subsidiary called Walmart Global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:42.300
Tech, formerly known as Walmart Labs, with&nbsp;
8000 employees. Why does a retailer need this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.220
many tech employees? Well, it’s because Walmart&nbsp;
doesn’t treat tech as an addition to their retail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:53.460
business, they treat tech as the basis of their&nbsp;
retail business. But, it wasn’t always like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:02:00.960
In fact, for nearly 16 years, Walmart had stalled&nbsp;
out. But since 2015, the retailer has tripled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.900
their market cap growing to a valuation of just&nbsp;
under $400 billion making them the 19th largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:12.600
company in the world. So, here’s the story of how&nbsp;
Walmart saved their stagnating business with tech.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:24.060
WALMART THE BOOMER:&nbsp;

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:43.800
Taking a look back, Walmart’s transition into&nbsp;
tech can be traced back to the late 1990s when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:49.200
Walmart was very much the boomers of the retail&nbsp;
industry. By this point, they were already the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.660
largest retailers in the US and the world’s&nbsp;
largest private employer. In fact, Walmart still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:00.360
holds this title to this day with a whopping&nbsp;
2.3 million employees. But, while things were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:06.480
going great at Walmart, they were starting a see&nbsp;
a little thorn pop up called Amazon, you may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.760
heard of them. At first, they started off as a no&nbsp;
name online bookstore, but every year, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.860
growing by hundreds if not thousands of percent.&nbsp;
They were expanding into new categories all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.500
time and it seemed like they wanted to be the&nbsp;
Walmart of the online world. In fact, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.660
starting to poach a bunch of Walmart employees by&nbsp;
luring them with sky high stock compensation. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:33.120
made it easy for Amazon to steal Walmart’s world&nbsp;
class logistics and supply chain secrets. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.160
when an up and coming competitor is able to steal&nbsp;
your employees and perform extraordinarily well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.020
it’s usually a sign that they’re doing something&nbsp;
that you’re not. With the case of Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:48.120
the X factor was obviously their online presence.&nbsp;
So, the best idea would’ve probabaly been for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:54.240
Walmart to poach some tech employees from Amazon&nbsp;
and double down on the online space. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.920
this is precisely what they’ve been doing over&nbsp;
the past several years. But, back in the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.960
Walmart would take the boomer approach and&nbsp;
try to bog down Amazon by suing them. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:09.300
that fiasco was going on at Walmart, our story&nbsp;
jumps over two tech enterpreneurs named Venky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.940
Harinarayan and Anand Rajaraman. These guys were&nbsp;
your classic tech guys from India having both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.280
completed degrees at IIT in India and Stanford&nbsp;
in America. They had gone on to create an online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.260
company called Junglee Corporation which allowed&nbsp;
shoppers to easily compares prices and features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:31.320
of various online products. This is nothing&nbsp;
crazy today, but back in the 1990s, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.840
revolutionary stuff and Jeff Bezos would take&nbsp;
interest in the company. In 1998, he would buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:42.960
out the Junglee for 1.6 million shares of Amazon&nbsp;
which at the time was worth $250 million. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:49.320
it’s worth $22 billion, so safe to say that these&nbsp;
two had scored a pretty lucrative deal but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:54.600
were by no means ready to retire. Both Venky and&nbsp;
Anand would join Amazon as high level directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.700
and closely work with Jeff Bezos himself. These&nbsp;
two would actually end up being instrumental in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.760
the launch of Amazon marketplace. You may not be&nbsp;
familiar with this brand but I’m sure you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.440
what they do. This is the part of Amazon that&nbsp;
allows for third party sellers to list products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.600
onto Amazon. As you know, this is basically&nbsp;
the bread and butter of Amazon today, so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.620
looks like Bezos definitely got his money’s worth&nbsp;
with this duo. Now, while that’s cool and all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.720
you’re probably wondering, what in the world&nbsp;
do these guys have to do with Walmart. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.280
these two are the ones that would create what&nbsp;
would eventually become Walmart Global Tech.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:34.500
WALMART FEELS THE PAIN:
After helping Bezos launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.820
Amazon marketplace, our duo would help&nbsp;
him launch Amazon Mechanical Turk. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.440
is basically the Fiverr of the corporate world.&nbsp;
Companies can outsource brute force work like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.900
data validation and content moderation through&nbsp;
Amazon. By the time Mechnical Turk came out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:54.300
Venky and Anand had spent the better part of a&nbsp;
decade at Amazon, and they started to feel that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:58.980
was time to move on. They didn’t directly switch&nbsp;
over to Walmart though. They would actually lean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:04.620
back into entrepreneurship and start another&nbsp;
company together called Kosmix. Kosmix was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:09.540
rather niche company but it was extremely valuable&nbsp;
for the enterprise customers that were in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:15.660
niche. What did Kosmix do you ask? Well, they&nbsp;
specialized in search engine optimization. More&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:20.340
specifically, they aimed to transform vertical&nbsp;
search engines into horizontal search engines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:25.320
Vertical searching is the most rudimentary type&nbsp;
of searching. It’s when people enter a keyword&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:30.120
and the engine searches a given database for&nbsp;
matches. Horizontal searching on the other hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.980
is more like the YouTube algorithm. It uses&nbsp;
the keyword that you entered along with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:39.420
previous searches and purchases to display the&nbsp;
products that you would be most likely to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:43.620
as opposed the products that are the closest&nbsp;
match to the keyword that you entered. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:50.340
standard stuff nowadays, but novel concept back in&nbsp;
the 2000s. Venky and Anand would go onto build an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:56.460
encyclopedia of nearly 5 million categories with&nbsp;
hundreds and hundred of parameters. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:00.480
they accounted for where customers were&nbsp;
searching from, what car did they drive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.280
what music they listen to, and even what type&nbsp;
of cheese they like. You could use all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.900
parameters to display products that would be&nbsp;
the most likely to convert into sales. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:15.780
for obvious reasons, this technology was extremely&nbsp;
valuable for retailers and they would eat it up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:20.580
Kosmix would end up growing hundreds of percent&nbsp;
per year, partner with several organizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:28.320
and even acquire smaller companies. So, Kosmix was&nbsp;
no doubt killing it but the same could not be said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:34.680
about Walmart. Walmart wasn’t exactly doing bad,&nbsp;
but they weren’t exactly doing well either. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:40.920
see, maturity had hit Walmart like a ton of bricks&nbsp;
and they were feeling the pain. During the 80s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:48.060
90s, Walmart had grown a ridiculous 60X putting&nbsp;
all other retailers to shame but for 10 years now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:53.520
Walmart had gone nowhere. If the industry as&nbsp;
a whole had stagnated, this wouldn’t be too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:59.760
big of a problem but e commerce was still growing&nbsp;
exponentially and Walmart was simply missing out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:06.900
They did have an online presence but compared to&nbsp;
Amazon, this was largely a joke. In fact, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:12.240
navigate back to their 2010 website, you’re hit&nbsp;
with a “Adobe Flash player is no longer supported”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:17.160
warning. Amazon on the other hand already had&nbsp;
a recommended section and was already evolving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:22.080
into its current form. Walmart clearly needed to&nbsp;
pivot but if they wanted to stage such a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.840
turnaround, they needed the help of experts. And&nbsp;
what better experts to hire than the former Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:33.120
directors that helped launch Amazon Marketplace&nbsp;
and were now working on a highly valuable search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:39.660
engine. And with that, Walmart would acquire&nbsp;
Kosmix for $300 million in cash in early 2011.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:46.080
WALMART PIVOTS:
It’s easy to say that Walmart just bought their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:52.200
way into tech but that’s not really the case. Yes,&nbsp;
they had a lot of help from Kosmix but Walmart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:57.120
deserves a bunch of credit as well. Usually&nbsp;
with acquisitions, companies try to integrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.860
the child company into the parent company. They&nbsp;
want the child company to adopt the same culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:07.380
the same philosophies, the same salary bands, and&nbsp;
so on. Walmart on the other hand decided to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:12.780
a much different approach. They could clearly tell&nbsp;
that what they were already doing wasn’t working.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:18.240
So, it doesn't make a whole lot of sense to force&nbsp;
this up and coming company to also do same thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:24.180
Instead, they would decide to adapt Walmart to&nbsp;
what Kosmix was doing. This takes an insane amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:30.000
of humility and most companies would never make&nbsp;
such a pivot but Walmart did. While all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.940
peers were paying legacy company tech salaries&nbsp;
like AT&amp;T and IBM, Walmart was paying FAANG level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:41.040
salaries or atleast as close as they could get.&nbsp;
Obviously, they would never be able to pay as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.500
as actual tech companies as they simply don’t have&nbsp;
the same margins. But, for a physical retailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:51.240
they were paying extraordinary amounts. This&nbsp;
allowed them to recruit some of the best talent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:55.320
out there including a bunch of Amazon employees&nbsp;
who were trying to escape the toxic work culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.320 --> 00:10:02.040
at Amazon. Suddenly, Walmart had all the resources&nbsp;
they needed to build a modern online platform and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:07.140
that’s exactly what they would do. But, they&nbsp;
didn’t just blindly copy Amazon. They did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.940
indeed add their own twist. One of the biggest&nbsp;
bottlenecks for Amazon is their fulfillment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:17.340
centers. To continue driving down delivery times,&nbsp;
Amazon has keep opening up fulfillment centers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:23.700
that are increasingly omnipresent. I mean, if you&nbsp;
wanna deliver within 24 hours to any address, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:30.240
need fulfillment centers within 24 hours of every&nbsp;
address. That’s kind of how that works. But, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.800
problem with opening so many fulfillment centers&nbsp;
is that you quickly reach the point of diminishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.780
returns. Opening more centers becomes more&nbsp;
expensive than its worth, but if you wanna keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:46.320
driving down delivery times to 2 hours and 1 hour,&nbsp;
that’s the only way. But see, this isn’t nearly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:53.160
big of an issue for Walmart because well, they’re&nbsp;
already omnipresent atleast within the US. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:59.220
fact, the average American lives within 4.2 miles&nbsp;
of a Walmart. So, forget 1 hour delivery, Walmart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:04.620
could do 15 minute delivery. All they needed to&nbsp;
do was convert their thousands of stores into high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:09.780
tech warehouses, and that’s exactly what they’ve&nbsp;
been doing. Walmart Global Tech could handle all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:14.520
of the AI and machine learning involved in running&nbsp;
such a large retail operation. What products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:19.020
needed to be carried by which stores and when.&nbsp;
What routes do their trucks need to make to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:24.540
that every store has what they need and so on and&nbsp;
so forth. Meanwhile, Walmart’s legacy business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:29.520
could focus on logistics, fulfillment, and&nbsp;
customer satisfaction. This strategy was already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:35.280
working out well in the 2010s, but the pandemic&nbsp;
changed things up big time. You see, Walmart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:40.740
had one thing that Amazon is still struggling to&nbsp;
offer: fresh groceries for pick up and delivery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:46.380
And this was exactly the advantage Walmart needed&nbsp;
to cement themselves as an ecommerce juggernaut.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.980
THE TECH WALMART:
Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:55.920
Walmart has been able to regain the momentum that&nbsp;
they had lost in the 2000s. Over the past 7 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:00.900
their stock has tripled and their fundamentals&nbsp;
have led the way. In fact, Walmart is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:07.320
number one revenue driver in the entire world&nbsp;
pulling in a crazy $600 billion per year. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:13.080
I do want to note that their earnings have not&nbsp;
kept up with their revenue growth but this isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:17.580
really a concern. The main reason for this is&nbsp;
simply that Walmart has been investing much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:22.500
their profits into the tech side of the business&nbsp;
and revenue growth. And for modern companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:28.920
growth is simply way more valuable than the bottom&nbsp;
line. Amazon for example is still not profitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:34.320
but they’re worth over a trillion dollars. So,&nbsp;
it looks like Walmart has not only adapted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:40.620
modern tech but they’ve adapted to ther modern&nbsp;
business landscape. And that is why Walmart is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:45.120
so successful to this day. Did you realize how&nbsp;
much Walmart was paying their tech workers?&nbsp;&nbsp;

